  {
    "episode_id": 71132,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71132.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 8,
    "turn_text": "So we absolutely. So you asked what do we need? I guess I should answer the question. We really need to come out of obscurity in terms of our need. My dad was one of the migrants that came in the 50s to take advantage of the industrialization of Wisconsin. And he had a third grade education. It was functionally illiterate. And when I think about just here in Milwaukee, for example, that there were 70,000 jobs lost in just a 30-year period of time that had workers like my dad. making middle class wages. So we need educational opportunity in the brand new economy that Milwaukee is creating. We need healthcare because obviously so many of these industrial jobs were connected, had healthcare connected with it. I was born, my dad had insurance. when I was born. There was no Medicaid. Mike, you know, was paid through my father's health insurance with J.I. Case. We need housing. I mean, surely 48% of Wisconsinites and probably a disproportionate number of black people are not only rent burdened meaning that they pay more than 30% of their income for housing, really ridiculous amounts, 50%, 75% of their income for housing and face high risks of homelessness because of it. But even when they pay their rent, they're living in some standard housing, housing that is filled with lead pay, you know, 65, 70% of these houses were built before 1960 and even more. were built, like the house I live in, built in 1920. So there are all kinds of problems with lead and other infrastructure problems which no landlord can afford and nobody can afford. And the loss of that manufacturing base, African Americans have been put at a real hard, created a real hardship. And a fourth of our kids Go to bed hungry every night in the city of Milwaukee. I'm choking up to even fear myself. Thank this.",
