  {
    "episode_id": 71132,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71132.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, no, those are some major problems. So you brought up segregation in Milwaukee. Milwaukee's been quoted as the most segregated city in the United States for a long time. Do you think we're seeing any progress on improving that segregation issue in Milwaukee?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Segregation in Milwaukee is a significant problem.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "Milwaukee has been described as the most segregated city in the United States.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "The claim about Milwaukee being the most segregated city has persisted over time.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes there may be progress toward reducing segregation in Milwaukee.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Segregation is measurable and objectively definable using established criteria.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Progress on segregation issues should be expected and possible given policy efforts.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The audience is familiar with or receptive to discussions about racial segregation in urban areas.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Public recognition of a problem implies responsibility to address it.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The credibility of the 'most segregated city' label depends on reliable data or authoritative sources.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Clarification Request (Specific)"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 71132,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71132.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "Well, I'll tell you, you know, you know, really old habits die hard. I mean, we still are suffering from the effects of redlining and the confidence that it does and does not bring to certain neighborhoods. Housing, prices, home ownership among black people is lower than it's ever been right now. And when we move to a different neighborhood, we have to pay premium prices. And then people complain that when black people move in, their housing values decrease. And I think that for a very short period of time, there was a thriving black middle class in Milwaukee. And I do think that when our incomes are commensurate or are on par with others in this region, we can not only bring housing assets back, but when they begin to appreciate to stay in our community, because the other dilemma is the extent to which housing is rehabilitated, then it's gentrified so that we can't afford it because the mismatch with our incomes So it is definitely something that should be within the scope of public policy to try to make interventions, to make property affordable, and so that communities don't have to really change their characteristics in order to be valuable.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Redlining continues to negatively affect certain neighborhoods.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "Public policy intervention is necessary to ensure housing affordability.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Housing prices in Black communities are currently at historically low levels relative to other groups.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Black families face higher costs when moving to new neighborhoods.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Housing rehabilitation frequently leads to gentrification beyond affordability for original residents.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "When Black people move into a neighborhood, housing values often decline according to some observers.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "There was once a brief period of significant Black middle-class growth in Milwaukee.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "If Black incomes match regional averages, homeownership and housing asset appreciation could improve.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Historical systemic discrimination like redlining has lasting economic consequences.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
