  {
    "episode_id": 71132,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71132.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "Well, the thing is, I'm the first African-American. And I never wanted to just have this job so I could be the first. And I certainly don't want to be the last. Because this is the continuum. I think Coretta Scott King said it best at one point. Every single generation. has to fight the same fights. I'm not discouraged because we haven't gotten everything done. I know that there's somebody that's going to come behind me to pick up the pieces, but in the meantime, I brought the select committee on economic despair and opportunity and growth to Milwaukee because I do want to continue to do things like enhancing the low income housing tax credits. I haven't given up on influencing the state legislature so that they can change the clause in the state constitution that doesn't allow us to provide differentials and property taxes to save communities. I haven't given up on to the extent we've got these new monies that I helped craft in the Ways and Means Committee for infrastructure improvements. I am so glad that a lot of those monies are going to be leveled toward a changing out lead pipes and lead paint. I think that this decreases the cost of housing for people who are trying to purchase it to rent it and make it affordable, as well as for people to purchase it and to stay in their own homes. So whoever is coming, they got to care about what's important in Milwaukee.",
