  {
    "episode_id": 71132,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71132.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "Well, hopefully it won't become as bad as it got in 2011 when we are actually downgraded. Our credit rating was downgraded for the first time in the history of the United States of America since Alexander Hamilton. became the first treasury secretary. And I mean, you know, just being a human being, you know, when your credit gets downrated, graded, it means that everything you purchase costs more and you have less access to capital. And our treasury bills are Class A everywhere in the world. And just the threat in 2011 of defaulting brought our credit rating down. You know, I think that Republicans, notably, I mean, we have raised, Democrats have raised the debt ceiling, and it was nothing but a yawn all the years that we've ever had a Republican president. It's Republicans that want to use it as a weapon. to try to force us to reduce spending. Now you ask them, okay, where do you want to make those cuts? You want to make them in Social Security and Medicare? They deny, even though their members say often as recently as yesterday or day before that they want to do it, they say no. You ask them, do they want to cut a defense spending? Absolutely not. Well, there's really nothing else left. After that, I mean, you're going to be cutting into bone and sinew and muscle at some point. Their tax cut in 2017, I mean, it was a giveaway. to billionaires. It really was. And whatever claims that they made about helping the middle class were minuscule in comparison to the giveaways they gave to corporate America. And so are they ready to resend? Are they ready to do more to capture more income at the top? They haven't told us. They don't want to raise revenue. Are they want to cut? What is it that they want to cut, wick? Food staffs, they changed the name of the subcommittee on which I am a member, the Work and Family Support Subcommittee, which has under it foster care and visiting home visiting for new moms, but it does have TANF under it. And they've changed that subcommittee title from Work and family support to work to welfare, to work in welfare. And so that gives me a lot of insight into their governing strategy, you know, is to just pass dispersions on any, any sort of program that's designed to help families so they can justify cutting it.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
